<h2>The grievance: three slices of interest</h2>
<p>The review applicant was the decree-holder. His complaint targeted one paragraph of the appellate judgment, contending it had wrongly awarded no interest for the pre-institution, pendente lite and post-decree periods — the three slices into which interest on a money decree conventionally divides. He invoked <em>Thazhathe Purayil Sarabi v. Union of India</em> for the entitlement to interest, and <em>B.N. Nobbis &amp; Co. v. Kamales Mallik</em> on maintainability of the review.</p>
<p>The respondent, though served, did not appear — and still won, because the application failed on its own terms.</p>

<h2>Why the review failed</h2>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><strong>1. No refusal to review.</strong> The judgment under review had not refused interest at all. It quantified the decretal sum with elaborate reasons, gave the judgment-debtor four weeks to pay without interest, and provided that interest at the awarded rate would revive on default. That is a payment-incentive structure, not an omission.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>2. The condition never triggered.</strong> Payment was tendered within the four weeks. The contingency that would have carried interest simply never arose — leaving nothing that could be called an error, let alone one apparent on the face of the record.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>3. Reappraisal is not review.</strong> What the applicant sought was a fresh evaluation of the evidence and the decree. A review bench does not sit in appeal over its own judgment; an arguable alternative view is a ground of appeal to a higher forum, not of review.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>4. The precedents did not fit.</strong> <em>B.N. Nobbis</em> concerned transfer of proceedings to the Commercial Division under the Commercial Courts Act, 2015 — a different question — and <em>Sarabi</em> could not displace a reasoned, specific finding on interest.</div>
</div>

<h2>Interest structured as an incentive</h2>
<p>The underlying judgment&rsquo;s device deserves attention in its own right. Courts increasingly use conditional interest — pay within a window and carry none; default and carry it at the full rate — to make decrees self-executing. For judgment-debtors, the message is that prompt compliance has a price advantage. For decree-holders, the lesson is to read such structures carefully before challenging them: a conditional waiver that achieves payment within weeks may be worth more than years of contested interest arithmetic.</p>
<div class="note">Review in commercial matters follows the same discipline as elsewhere: an error apparent on the face of the record, discovery of new and important matter not available earlier despite due diligence, or other sufficient reason of the same species. Anything more ambitious belongs in an appeal — here, to the Supreme Court — not in a review application before the same bench.</div>

<h2>Checklist before filing a review</h2>
<div class="check">
  <p>Identify the precise error and test it: can it be demonstrated without re-arguing the case? If it needs submissions, it is not apparent on the face of the record.</p>
  <p>Confirm the judgment actually decided the point against you — a conditional or contingent structure, as here, is not a refusal.</p>
  <p>Account for limitation, and support any condonation application with specific cause; this Bench condoned 37 days, but condonation is grace, not right.</p>
  <p>Weigh the alternative remedies first — a review dismissed on merits can complicate the record for a later challenge to the main judgment.</p>
</div>
